    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d101-a1">

                <pb facs="0247" n="191" xml:id="pg191"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >South Wales Atlantic Steamship Company</hi>, (<hi
                                rendition="#small-caps">Limited</hi>,)<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Cardiff</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-02-23"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 23,
                        1875</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reply to your communication of the 19th
                    instant, addressed to the secretary of this company, we, the undersigned
                    commanders of the steamships Glamorgan and Pembroke, taking your questions in
                    the order you present them, beg to state we are acquainted with the
                    sound-signals used on shipboard in the United States, and consider they greatly
                    conduce to safety.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>The draft-rule in your letter of the 19th instant virtually embodies the
                        system in use in the United States, and we believe its adoption would be a
                        great benefit to the royal navy and mercantile marine.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The application and interpretation could not be more easy or
                        simple.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Most decidedly, and the more intricate the navigation the more
                        advantageous it would be.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>Quite sufficient.</item>
                    <label>5.</label>
                    <item>No.</item>
                    <label>6.</label>
                    <item>We cannot see any reason why it should be restricted to foggy weather; to
                        do so would lessen its advantages. We have now occasionally to vary from the
                        usual custom of porting according to article 19, and it certainly would
                        greatly relieve anxiety and conduce to safety if we could be assured by
                        sound that an approaching ship appreciated our position and intentions.
                        Sounds should indicate intended movements as well in clear as in foggy
                        weather, and particularly in narrow waters.</item>
                </list>
                <p>In conclusion, we respectfully suggest that the vessel first signalizing by
                    sounds should have the right of way. This is the custom in the United
                    States.</p>
                <p>We are, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed><list>
                            <item>JOSEPH LAYBOURNE,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Commander Steamship
                                    Glamorgan</hi>.</item>
                            <item>H. C. WILLIAMS,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Commander Steamship
                                    Pembroke.</hi></item>
                        </list>
                    </signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>The <hi rendition="#small-caps">Assistant Secretary</hi><lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Board of Trade, Marine Department</hi>.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
